<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  NOTHING LEAVES THE DEVICE, INCLUDING TO ANOTHER DEVICE.

  android:allowBackup="false" is NOT enough here. Android's own Auto
  Backup guide says so, for exactly this app's targetSdkVersion:

      "Note: For apps targeting Android 12 (API level 31) or higher, this
       behavior varies. On devices from some device manufacturers,
       specifying android:allowBackup="false" disables cloud-based backup
       and restore (such as Google Drive backups) but doesn't disable
       device-to-device transfers for the app."

  This application targets 36. So on some manufacturers' devices the
  manifest's stated intent - back nothing up - was being honoured for
  Google Drive and quietly ignored for a device-to-device transfer, which
  would copy this app's private storage onto a new phone. That storage
  holds the operator's board configuration and whatever firmware file
  they last opened.

  Android Lint reported the gap as DataExtractionRules; this file is the
  answer to it, not a suppression of it.

  EVERY EXCLUDE CARRIES path=".".

  The guide's syntax block gives path as part of both elements:

      <exclude domain=["file" | "database" | "sharedpref" | "external" |
                       "root" | "device_file" | "device_database" |
                       "device_sharedpref" | "device_root" ]
               path="string" />

  and its own examples spell "the whole domain" as path="." - e.g.
  <include domain="sharedpref" path="."/> is described as backing up all
  shared preferences. The first version of this file left path off
  entirely. Lint did not object, which is not the same as the platform
  honouring it, and an exclude the parser drops is not an exclusion at
  all - it is this file appearing to do its job while the data goes
  across anyway. path="." removes the question.

  AN ABSENT SECTION IS NOT A DENIAL. It is the opposite:

      "If there are no rules for a particular backup mode, such as if the
       <device-transfer> section is missing, that mode is fully enabled
       for all content except for no-backup and cache directories, as
       described in the Files that are backed up section."

  That sentence is why <device-transfer> is written out in full below
  rather than left implicit - and it is also why the earlier comment
  here, which claimed the absence of <cross-platform-transfer> was a
  deliberate refusal, was simply wrong. Absence enables. See
  res/xml-v36/data_extraction_rules.xml, which carries the
  <cross-platform-transfer> section; the split by API level is explained
  there.

  Every domain the schema defines is excluded from every section present,
  so the rule is "nothing", not "nothing except what I forgot to list".
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup disableIfNoEncryptionCapabilities="true">
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
        <exclude domain="external" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
